from pathlib import Path
Path('/app/answer.json').write_text('{"answer": "The test verifies that the application logger\'s handlers list is empty because Flask\'s logger initialization only adds its default handler when no handler exists in the logging hierarchy that can handle the logger\'s effective level.\\n\\nIn `src/flask/logging.py`, the `create_logger` function (called when accessing `app.logger`) checks `has_level_handler(logger)` before adding the default handler:\\n\\n```python\\nif not has_level_handler(logger):\\n    logger.addHandler(default_handler)\\n```\\n\\nThe `has_level_handler` function walks up the logging hierarchy (checking the logger and its parent loggers) to see if any handler can handle the logger\'s effective level. When a stream handler is added to the root logger before accessing `app.logger`, `has_level_handler` returns `True` because the root logger is a parent in the hierarchy, so Flask does not add its default handler.\\n\\nThis avoids duplicate handlers and respects existing logging configuration. The test in `tests/test_logging.py` (lines 48-51) confirms this: after adding a handler to the root logger, `app.logger.handlers` is empty because Flask detected an existing handler in the hierarchy and skipped adding its own.\\n\\nThis design allows Flask to integrate with existing logging setups without forcing its own handler when one already exists."}' + '\n')
